=== CRITERION 2 — Year >= ProjectionYears contributes 0 ===
PASS.
Test exists: TestPresentValue_OneTimeExpense_AtOrBeyondHorizonContributesNothing
(present_value_one_time_test.go:90-111), subtests year=10 (== horizon) and
year=15 (> horizon) against ProjectionYears=10, asserting |diff| <= 1e-9.
Passes — see criterion 1(d) output.
MUTATION PROOF: replacing `continue` with `chargeMonth = months - 1` (clamp
instead of skip) in $SP/z1 gives
    --- FAIL: .../#00  year=10: PVExpenses diff = 24751.442307896, want 0
    --- FAIL: .../#01  year=15: PVExpenses diff = 28693.705369076, want 0
So the horizon guard is genuinely asserted, not vacuous.
NOTE (disclosed, not a defect): this is a negative test, so it necessarily also
passes on a featureless tree — under mutation A (whole leg deleted) it PASSED
while tests 1 and 2 FAILED. Its value is proven only by mutation D above.

=== CRITERION 3 — zero discount rate: PV == inflated amount exactly ===
PASS.
Test exists: TestPresentValue_OneTimeExpense_ZeroDiscountRate
(present_value_one_time_test.go:59-85), sets DiscountRate = 0 on both fixtures
and asserts |diff - amount*inflationFactor| <= 1e-6 (absolute, no discounting).
Passes — see criterion 1(d).
Implementation path confirmed: MonthlyCompoundFactorFromDecimal(0) returns 1.0
(engine/math.go:7-11, `if annualRate == 0 { return 1.0 }`), so monthlyRate == 0
and presentValueOfMonthlyStream takes the `else { pv += amt }` branch (line 209).
MUTATION PROOF: mutation (f) above makes exactly this test fail
    PVExpenses diff = 20000.000000, want 22510.176200
demonstrating it pins the inflated amount, not the raw amount.

=== CRITERION 4 — test (a) recomputes expectations independently ===
PASS.
Read present_value_one_time_test.go:43-47. The expectation is built from literal
formulas, not from a second call to PresentValue:
    chargeMonth := year * 12
    monthlyRate := engine.MonthlyCompoundFactorFromDecimal(withOne.DiscountRate/100) - 1
    inflationFactor := math.Pow(1+withOne.InflationRate/100, float64(chargeMonth)/12.0)
    inflatedAmount := amount * inflationFactor
    want := inflatedAmount / math.Pow(1+monthlyRate, float64(chargeMonth+1))
PresentValue is called twice only to form the with/without differential fixture;
`want` is never derived from its output. The inflation factor is re-derived
literally rather than by calling engine.compoundedFactorFromPercent.

The one production helper reused is engine.MonthlyCompoundFactorFromDecimal (a
3-line wrapper, engine/math.go:7-20). To prove that reuse masks nothing I wrote
a throwaway probe in $SP/z1solo using ONLY literal math
(mr := math.Pow(1+DiscountRate/100, 1.0/12.0) - 1) and brute-forcing the whole
leg from scratch across five fixtures:
    $ go test ./internal/services/retirement/analysis/ -count=1 -run TestCheckerProbe -v
    year0:          got=12294.909019 want=12294.909019
    year1:          got=8792.748682  want=8792.748682
    year29_last:    got=22807.834861 want=22807.834861
    same_year_pair: got=45231.798620 want=45231.798620
    two_years:      got=31163.632045 want=31163.632045
    --- PASS: TestCheckerProbe_OneTimeLegMatchesBruteForce (0.00s)
All five agree to <=1e-6 relative. Probe file deleted after the run (it lived
only in the scratch copy; the real tree was never touched).
Bonus finding from that probe: `same_year_pair` (30k + 20k both in year 5)
yields 45231.798620 — identical to the single 50k-in-year-5 figure — so the
group-by-year map does prevent the double-count its comment claims.

